{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310649,"nonce":"35XA5xU+1PeOjikA6m1b7O70nb8=","title":"Map.keys() SIGTRAPs (exit 133, no diagnostic) at HEAD — StringMap is extern, keys() has no body and no mapping entry, so the safety net ...","message":"**REPRODUCED 2026-08-18 at `884dfaf9`.** Supersedes the vaguer note that\n`Map.keys()` gives \"can't resolve symbol keys\" — the real failure is a SILENT\ncrash.\n\n    var m = new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e(); m.set(\"a\",1);\n    var it = m.keys();              // exit=133, NO output, NO diagnostic\n\n**exit 133 = 128+5 = SIGTRAP.** Merely CALLING `keys()` traps; you do not need\nto iterate it. Isolated against the same build:\n\n| expression | result |\n|---|---|\n| `m.get(\"a\")` | works |\n| `for (k in m)` | works (iterates KEYS — see [[bugs_map_for_in_iterates_keys]]) |\n| `m.keys()` | **exit 133** |\n| `for (k in m.keys())` | **exit 133** |\n\n**ROOT CAUSE — a mapping gap, not codegen.** `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` prints:\n\n    [skip-stub:fwdref]      IrFunctionId(6) haxe.ds.StringMap.keys\n    [safety-net:fwdref-stub] IrFunctionId(6) haxe.ds.StringMap.keys\n\n`haxe-std/haxe/ds/StringMap.hx:32` is `extern class StringMap\u003cT\u003e`, so\n`keys():Iterator\u003cString\u003e` (line 64) is a bodiless declaration — and\n`compiler/src/stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs` (~2845) maps only `new`, `set`, `get`,\n`exists`, `remove`, `clear`, `toString`. **No `keys` entry**, so it degrades to a\nforward-ref stub and the finalizer installs a `udf` trap.\n\n**THE RUNTIME SIDE ALREADY EXISTS** — nothing needs writing there:\n- `haxe_stringmap_keys(map, out_len) -\u003e *mut *mut HaxeString` (raw array + out-param)\n- `haxe_stringmap_keys_to_array(map) -\u003e *mut HaxeArray` (**a real Haxe Array**)\nBoth are registered in `runtime/src/plugin_impl.rs`.\n\n**THE DESIGN DECISION THAT BLOCKS A ONE-LINE FIX:** the declared return type is\n`Iterator\u003cString\u003e` but every available runtime symbol returns an ARRAY. Options,\nnone free:\n1. map `keys` → `haxe_stringmap_keys_to_array` AND change the haxe-std signature\n   to `Array\u003cString\u003e` — breaks `IMap` conformance and `var it:Iterator\u003cString\u003e =\n   m.keys()`, and any haxe-std edit forces a snapshot regen.\n2. give `keys()` an `@:runtime inline` body returning `keysArray().iterator()`,\n   with a new mapped `keysArray()`. **Precedent exists in the same file**:\n   `keyValueIterator` at line 78 is exactly this shape — but it is gated\n   `#if eval`, so it does not apply on rayzor today.\n3. teach the compiler to special-case `keys()` the way it already special-cases\n   Map `for-in` (which is why `for (k in m)` works while `keys()` does not).\n\nOption 2 looks best — it keeps the Haxe API honest and reuses an existing\npattern. NOT attempted.\n\n**WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ergonomics:** it rules out enumerating a `StringMap`'s\nkeys, e.g. reading all `\u003carch\u003e.*` GGUF metadata keys generically out of\n`GGUFReader.meta`. Work around it by reading NAMED keys (what\n`GGUFLoader.metadataFromReader` already does), not by iterating.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_map_keys_traps_exit_133` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}